✅ Huffman Encoding — Theory Using Greedy Method

Huffman Encoding is a compression technique that assigns shorter codes to more frequent characters and longer codes to less frequent characters, minimizing the total cost of encoding.

It is a classic example of using a greedy strategy to build an optimal prefix-free code.

✅ 1. Purpose of Huffman Coding

Given characters with frequencies (or probabilities), we want to construct a binary tree such that:

Frequent characters get shorter binary codes

Infrequent characters get longer binary codes

No code is a prefix of another → prefix-free

Goal: Minimize the total weighted path length
Σ (frequency × code length)

✅ 2. Why Huffman Is a Greedy Strategy

At every step, we:

✅ Pick the two characters with the smallest frequencies
✅ Combine them to form a new node whose frequency is the sum
✅ Insert this back into the list and repeat

This local choice of combining the two least frequent symbols leads to the globally optimal prefix-free code.

That is exactly what a greedy algorithm does.

✅ 3. Huffman Encoding Algorithm (Greedy)
Step 1: Input

List of characters with their frequencies.

Step 2: Create Leaf Nodes

Each character becomes a leaf node of a binary tree.

Step 3: Build a Min-Heap (Priority Queue)

Insert all nodes into a min-heap sorted by frequency.

Step 4: Construct the Huffman Tree

Repeat until only one node remains:

Extract the two nodes with the minimum frequency.

Create a new internal node whose frequency = sum of both.

Make the two nodes left and right children.

Insert the new node back into the min-heap.

The final node remaining is the root of the Huffman tree.

Step 5: Generate Codes

Traverse the tree:

Left edge → ‘0’

Right edge → ‘1’

The leaf nodes receive their codewords.

✅ 4. Example (Concept Only, No Code)

If frequencies are like:

Char	Freq
A	5
B	9
C	12
D	13
E	16
F	45

Huffman will first combine the smallest (A, B) → new node 14,
then combine 12 and 13 → new node 25, etc.

Greedy choice happens at every merge step.

✅ 5. Why Huffman Coding Is Optimal

Because:

It is based on optimal merge pattern

It always merges the two smallest frequency nodes

Proven using the greedy-choice property and optimal substructure

Thus, Huffman coding always gives the minimum weighted path length.

✅ 6. Time Complexity Analysis

The main operations happen in the min-heap.

Let:

n = number of unique characters

✅ Heap operations:

Building the heap → O(n)

Extraction (n − 1 times) → each O(log n)

Insertion (n − 1 times) → each O(log n)

✅ Total Time:
O(n log n)


This is the standard complexity of Huffman coding.

✅ 7. Space Complexity Analysis

Space is used to store:

All tree nodes (leaf + internal)

The min-heap structure

The final Huffman tree

Number of total nodes ≈ 2n − 1

Thus:

✅ Space Complexity: O(n)

If you include code storage (binary strings), it becomes:

✅ Worst-case: O(n × L)
Where L = maximum code length.

But algorithmically, we consider:

✅ Huffman Algorithm Space: O(n)

✅ 8. Exam-Ready Summary / Practical Write-Up

Huffman coding is a greedy algorithm used for optimal prefix-free encoding. It constructs a binary tree by repeatedly selecting the two symbols with the smallest frequencies, merging them into a new node, and reinserting the node into a min-heap. This continues until a single tree remains. Traversing the tree assigns binary codes to characters, ensuring shorter codes for more frequent characters and minimizing total encoding cost.

Time Complexity: O(n log n) due to heap operations

Space Complexity: O(n) for storing the tree and priority queue

Greedy Strategy: Always combine the two least frequent nodes

Output: Optimal prefix-free binary codes for each character
